# Python Code Style
Rule Type: Always

## General Python Style Rules
- Maximum line length: 100 characters
- Indent size: 4 spaces
- Use spaces, not tabs
- Quote style: Double quotes
- Docstring style: Google
- Class docstrings: Required
- Function docstrings: Required
- Type hints: Required

## Import Rules
- Order imports in the following sequence:
  1. Standard library imports
  2. Third-party imports
  3. Local application imports
- Group imports by package
- Sort imports within each group
- Use absolute imports for local modules
- Avoid wildcard imports (*)

## Code Organization
- Maximum function length: 50 lines
- Maximum class length: 300 lines
- Maximum file length: 1000 lines
- Maximum cyclomatic complexity: 10 per function
- Maximum number of arguments: 5 per function
- Maximum number of return statements: 3 per function

## Naming Conventions
- Classes: PascalCase
- Functions and variables: snake_case
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Private members: _prefix_snake_case
- Protected members: _prefix_snake_case
- Module names: lowercase_snake_case

## Documentation
- All public APIs must have docstrings
- Docstrings must follow Google style
- Include type hints for all function parameters and return values
- Document all exceptions that may be raised
- Include usage examples for complex functions

## Code Comments
- Use comments to explain why, not what
- Keep comments up to date with code changes
- Use TODO comments for future improvements
- Document complex algorithms with clear explanations
- Avoid commented-out code

## Error Handling
- Use specific exception types
- Include meaningful error messages
- Handle exceptions at appropriate levels
- Clean up resources in finally blocks
- Log all exceptions with appropriate context

## Testing
- Write unit tests for all public functions
- Use descriptive test names
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
- Mock external dependencies
- Test edge cases and error conditions
